      "claim": "In monkeys, [11C]MePPEP showed high brain uptake reaching almost 600% SUV within 10–20 min after systemic administration, directly demonstrating meaningful CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C12"],
      "claim": "In rats, BMS-204352 crossed the blood-brain barrier with brain-to-plasma ratios of approximately 7–11, indicating substantial brain exposure after systemic dosing.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "In humans, penicillin G CSF concentrations were significantly below 1% of serum levels, indicating poor CNS access under normal barrier conditions.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Pass: multiple direct in-vivo brain uptake and high B/P studies across species support meaningful CNS access; the human CSF negative for penicillin G is a contradictory signal but does not override the consistent positive direct CNS exposure evidence.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Most positive evidence is from rodent/monkey brain uptake; no human CSF or clinical CNS exposure data are available for those query-related molecules.",
    "Molecule 1 has conflicting CSF results depending on meningeal inflammation; normal-barrier human data are negative, and inflamed-meninges positive data are discounted as disrupted-barrier evidence."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

